CAT = "rdEducation"; SEG = "EDU"
Q = [
("A client has bought measuring cups and plans to start logging meals next week. Which stage of change is this?",
 "Preparation", ["Contemplation", "Action", "Maintenance"],
 "Preparation means intending to act within about a month and already taking small steps. Contemplation is weighing change without a plan; action is the first months of doing it."),
("A client in precontemplation is referred for weight counseling. What approach fits this stage best?",
 "Raise awareness of the issue", ["Set a detailed weekly meal plan", "Plan for relapse triggers", "Track daily food intake"],
 "In precontemplation the client does not yet see a problem, so the aim is to build awareness and explore concerns without pressure. Action plans and self-monitoring suit later stages."),
("In motivational interviewing, what does the R in OARS stand for?",
 "Reflective listening", ["Rapid goal setting", "Reassuring the client", "Repeating instructions"],
 "OARS is open questions, affirmations, reflections and summaries. Reflective listening restates what the client means and invites them to keep exploring."),
("Which is an open-ended question for a diet history?",
 "What does a usual day look like?", ["Do you eat breakfast daily?", "Is lunch your biggest meal?", "Do you drink regular soda?"],
 "Open questions cannot be answered with yes or no and draw out detail. The other three are closed questions, useful for checking specific facts."),
("A client says, 'I really want to be able to play with my grandkids.' In motivational interviewing, what is this?",
 "Change talk", ["Sustain talk", "Discord", "A righting reflex"],
 "Change talk is the client's own reasons, desire or ability to change, and the counselor reinforces it. Sustain talk argues for staying the same; the righting reflex is the counselor's urge to fix things."),
("Under the health belief model, a client who thinks diabetes is serious but sees no personal risk lacks which belief?",
 "Perceived susceptibility", ["Perceived severity", "Perceived benefits", "Cues to action"],
 "Perceived susceptibility is believing one could get the condition; perceived severity is believing it would be serious. Both, with benefits that outweigh barriers, raise the chance of action."),
("A teen gains confidence in cooking vegetables after watching a peer do it successfully. Which theory explains this?",
 "Social cognitive theory", ["Stages of change model", "Theory of reasoned action", "Diffusion of innovations"],
 "Social cognitive theory includes observational learning and self-efficacy: seeing a similar person succeed raises one's own confidence. Reciprocal determinism links person, behavior and environment."),
("Teaching a patient to draw up and inject insulin targets which learning domain?",
 "Psychomotor", ["Cognitive", "Affective", "Interpersonal"],
 "Psychomotor learning is physical skill, best taught by demonstration and return demonstration. Cognitive learning is knowledge, and affective learning concerns attitudes and values."),
("Which behavioral objective is written correctly?",
 "Client will list 3 iron-rich foods", ["Client will understand iron", "Client will learn about iron", "Client will value an iron-rich diet"],
 "A behavioral objective uses an observable, measurable verb (list, select, demonstrate) and a criterion. 'Understand' and 'learn' cannot be observed directly."),
("A dietitian checks learning by asking a patient to explain the low-potassium diet in her own words. What is this?",
 "Teach-back method", ["Return demonstration", "Summative evaluation", "Reflective listening"],
 "Teach-back confirms understanding by having the patient restate the instructions, and the educator re-teaches what was missed. Return demonstration is for physical skills."),
("Materials for a general adult audience are usually written at about which reading level?",
 "6th to 8th grade", ["10th to 12th grade", "College level", "3rd grade or below"],
 "Health literacy guidance recommends patient materials at or below about a 6th to 8th grade level, checked with a tool such as SMOG or Fry, plus plain words and white space."),
("A new class handout is tried with a small group and revised before the program launches. What evaluation is this?",
 "Formative", ["Summative", "Outcome", "Impact"],
 "Formative evaluation happens during development to improve materials and methods. Summative evaluation judges the finished program."),
("A program counts class attendance and checks that each session followed the curriculum. What evaluation is this?",
 "Process evaluation", ["Impact evaluation", "Outcome evaluation", "Cost evaluation"],
 "Process evaluation looks at delivery: reach, dose and fidelity. Impact evaluation measures short-term changes in knowledge or behavior, and outcome evaluation the long-term health results."),
("Three months after a cooking program, a survey shows families eat more vegetables. What does this measure?",
 "Program impact", ["Program process", "Program inputs", "Program outputs"],
 "Short- to medium-term changes in knowledge, attitudes or behavior are impact measures. Long-term health results such as lower BMI rates are outcomes."),
("According to adult learning principles, how should education for adults be designed?",
 "Around problems they face now", ["As lectures with no questions", "With the same content for all", "Around grades and tests"],
 "Knowles' andragogy holds that adults are self-directed, bring experience and want learning they can apply now. Problem-centered sessions that use their experience work best."),
("A group of new members is polite and unsure of its purpose. Which stage of group development is this?",
 "Forming", ["Storming", "Norming", "Performing"],
 "Tuckman's stages are forming, storming, norming and performing. Forming is the orientation stage; storming brings conflict as roles are tested."),
("A client keeps a food and mood log to spot when she eats out of boredom. Which cognitive behavioral strategy is this?",
 "Self-monitoring", ["Stimulus control", "Cognitive restructuring", "Contingency contract"],
 "Self-monitoring records behavior and its context to reveal patterns. Stimulus control changes cues in the environment, and cognitive restructuring challenges unhelpful thoughts."),
("A client keeps snack foods out of sight and off the desk. Which behavioral strategy is this?",
 "Stimulus control", ["Self-monitoring", "Cognitive restructuring", "Positive reinforcement"],
 "Stimulus control removes or changes cues that trigger the behavior. It is often paired with self-monitoring, which shows which cues matter."),
("A grocery store tour teaching how to compare unit prices and Nutrition Facts labels is which kind of activity?",
 "Experiential group education", ["One-on-one diet counseling", "Mass media campaign on TV", "Policy intervention"],
 "Grocery tours and cooking demonstrations let a group practice skills in a real setting. Mass media reaches many people with a message but little practice."),
("When a counselor says, 'Many people find it hard to give up sweets. What has been hard for you?', what is the counselor doing?",
 "Normalizing, then asking open", ["Giving advice without permission", "Confronting the client", "Using a leading question"],
 "Normalizing tells the client the struggle is common, which reduces defensiveness, and the open question invites the client to explore. A leading question suggests the answer."),
]
